  "Question": "A coin is tossed twice independently. The probability of heads on the first toss is \\(1/2\\), and the probability of heads on the second toss is \\(1/2\\). What is the probability of heads on both tosses?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "1/4",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Method 1: Multiply independent probabilities\n\nBecause the tosses are independent, multiply their heads probabilities:\n\\[\nP(\\text{heads on both tosses})=\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12=\\frac14.\n\\]\n\nMethod 2: List the ordered outcomes\n\nLet \\(H\\) denote heads and \\(T\\) denote tails. Since the tosses are independent and each has equally likely heads and tails, the four ordered outcomes\n\\[\nHH,\\quad HT,\\quad TH,\\quad TT\n\\]\nare equally likely. The letters record the first and second toss in order, so the list includes every possibility exactly once. Only \\(HH\\) has two heads, giving probability \\(\\frac14\\).",
